 ## Overview
-Ambient Light Approximation provides a computationally efficient method for simulating indirect sky illumination in participating media, specifically volumetric clouds, by integrating the sky hemisphere into a single upward-propagating light value [S1, unofficial]. Unlike full multiple-scattering solvers or complex spherical harmonic integrations, this method approximates the diffuse sky contribution using a single ray cast upwards from the sample point, weighted by local density and transmittance [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Lighting Models](2056-lighting-models.md) by explicitly handling the shadowing of ambient light through density integration, ensuring that deep or covered parts of a cloud receive less ambient fill than exposed edges [S1, unofficial].
+Ambient Light Approximation provides a computationally efficient estimate of indirect sky illumination in participating media, primarily volumetric clouds, by replacing the integration of the sky hemisphere with a single upward ray [S1, unofficial]. Unlike full path tracing or multi-scattering models, it simulates the shadowing effect of cloud density on ambient light without the noise or cost of random direction sampling [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Lighting Models](2056-lighting-models.md) by specifically addressing the "dark edge" and blue tint artifacts caused by ignoring indirect light, using a pre-integrated sky value modulated by local density [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method approximates the integral of incoming radiance from the sky hemisphere by assuming the majority of ambient light originates from directly above. It combines a pre-integrated sky color with a density-weighted attenuation factor derived from an upward ray.
+The method approximates the integral of sky radiance over the upper hemisphere by evaluating a single ray directed upwards from the shading point. This approach relies on the assumption that the sky is the dominant source of ambient light and that the "blurry" nature of volumetric scattering allows for significant simplification [S1, unofficial].
 
